# Stop a mouse cursor appearing on the panel.
#
# THE PROBLEM
# The PocketTerm35's RP2040 presents a COMPOSITE USB HID device: a keyboard
# collection (which is the only one Tracer uses — see tracerd/keymap.default.json,
# captured from hardware) and a mouse collection that reports nothing, because
# there is no pointing device on the unit. Touch arrives separately from the
# GT911 over I2C.
#
# That idle mouse collection is enough for libinput to enumerate a pointer, and
# a compositor with a pointer draws a cursor. On the panel that is a defect: the
# operator has no mouse, so an arrow sitting on screen is confusing and it
# obscures the UI.
#
# WHY CSS DOES NOT FIX IT
# tracer-ui already sets `cursor: none` unconditionally (tracer-ui/src/styles/
# tokens.css) and every element inherits it. That is a CLIENT instruction:
# Chromium can only act on it for its own surface, and only once the pointer has
# entered that surface. Nothing on this unit ever moves the pointer, so the
# compositor's own cursor can be drawn and never handed over to the client.
# The same CSS does hide the cursor under Raspberry Pi OS, where a real mouse is
# being moved — which is exactly why the CSS looked correct and was not enough.
#
# Removing the pointer at the source is the fix: no pointer device, no cursor,
# nothing for the compositor to draw.
#
# SAFETY
# Matched narrowly on purpose. This device also carries the keyboard, and every
# button on the unit arrives through it — ignoring the wrong interface would
# leave the operator with a device that cannot be navigated at all.
#
#   ID_INPUT_MOUSE=="1"      only an interface libinput sees as a pointer
#   ID_INPUT_KEYBOARD!="1"   never the keyboard, even if one node claims both
#   ID_INPUT_TOUCHSCREEN!="1" never the GT911; touch must keep working
#
# id/vendor and id/product are the input device's own attributes: four lowercase
# hex digits with no 0x prefix, so 0x1209/0x0001 is written 1209/0001.
#
# VERIFYING ON HARDWARE
# List what libinput actually sees, and confirm no pointer is present:
#   for d in /dev/input/event*; do udevadm info --query=property "$d" \
#     | grep -qE '^ID_INPUT_MOUSE=1' && echo "$d is a pointer"; done

SUBSYSTEM=="input", ATTRS{id/vendor}=="1209", ATTRS{id/product}=="0001", \
  ENV{ID_INPUT_MOUSE}=="1", ENV{ID_INPUT_KEYBOARD}!="1", \
  ENV{ID_INPUT_TOUCHSCREEN}!="1", ENV{LIBINPUT_IGNORE_DEVICE}="1"
